// Reading canonical RLP — on `core`'s cursor, which is where this file stops being self-contained.
//
// **This file is what the shipped one says it could not write.** Its `Cursor`:
//
// > A struct rather than a return value threaded through every helper, because a failure has to stop
// > the walk without unwinding — wac has no exceptions, and a recursive descent that returns an
// > error tuple at every level is where the checks get dropped.
//
// *wac has no exceptions* is true and is still true. What the shipped file did not have is `try`,
// which is neither an exception nor a tuple at every level: the propagation is written once per
// call, in one token, and a call whose failure is **not** handled does not compile. So the thing the
// comment was afraid of — a dropped check — stops being a discipline and becomes a type error.
//
// The cost of the sticky field, written out, is what this replaces:
//
//   * `if (c.error != "") { … }` appears **five times** — `item`'s entry guard, twice after
//     `longLength` in that same function, once after `item` inside `list`, and once in `decode`;
//   * `item` needs the entry guard at all because it may be re-entered after a failure it cannot
//     see, which is the cost of a channel the type does not mention;
//   * and each failure path has to invent an `Item` to return, so `Item.Bytes(u8[0]())` is written
//     **four times** as a value that means nothing and is never read.
//
// That last one is the tell. A function that must produce a value it does not have is a function
// whose return type is wrong, and `Result` is the type that says so.
//
// ## And it declared its own cursor until 2026-09-05, which is why it is the first consumer
//
// `../../../core/cursor.wac` was written after a sweep found `Reader` in five shipped packages, and
// its last paragraph named this file: *"`@/packages/rlp/src/decode.wac` declares one here — in a
// directory that has `Bytes` and spent three days arguing for it."* This is the follow-through, and
// **three of the cursor's own predictions came true and one did not.** §1 below.

import { Result, Bytes, Vec, Cursor, Overrun } from "core";
import { Item } from "./item.wac";
import {
  RlpFault, NotSelfEncoded, LeadingZero, NotMinimal, TooLong, ListOverrun, Trailing,
} from "./fault.wac";

/**
 * The one item this input encodes.
 *
 * Trailing bytes are a failure: RLP is canonical, and an input that encodes an item *and then some*
 * has two readings, which is the shape of several real Ethereum vulnerabilities.
 */
export Result<Item, RlpFault> decode(Bytes input) {
  Cursor c = Cursor.of(input);
  Item it = try item(c);
  if (!c.empty()) { return Err(Trailing(c.at, c.left())); }
  return Ok(it);
}

/**
 * One item at the cursor.
 *
 * **A free function taking a `Cursor`, where it was a method on this file's own `Reader`.** wac has
 * no extension methods, so a package that adopts a `core` type has to write its own operations
 * beside it rather than on it — see §1.
 *
 * The order of the tests is the order the format is defined in, and the first one matters: a
 * decoder gets it wrong by treating `0x00..0x7f` as a zero-length prefix.
 */
Result<Item, RlpFault> item(Cursor c) {
  // An `i32`, as the original has it: `tag - 0x80` on a `u8` is a subtraction at the wrong width,
  // and every branch below is an arithmetic one.
  i32 tag = (try c.u8()) as i32;

  if (tag < 0x80)  { return Ok(Str(one(tag as@ u8))); }
  if (tag <= 0xB7) { return Ok(Str(try shortString(c, tag - 0x80))); }
  if (tag <= 0xBF) { return Ok(Str(try c.take(try longLength(c, tag - 0xB7)))); }
  if (tag <= 0xF7) { return list(c, tag - 0xC0); }
  return list(c, try longLength(c, tag - 0xF7));
}

/** `n` bytes in the ≤55 form, where a one-byte payload has a canonical rule. */
Result<Bytes, RlpFault> shortString(Cursor c, i32 n) {
  i32 at = c.at;
  Bytes out = try c.take(n);
  // `0x81 0x05` spells a byte that spells itself. Canonical RLP has one encoding per value and this
  // is the second one, so it is invalid input rather than a tolerated variant.
  if (n == 1 && out.get(0) < 0x80) { return Err(NotSelfEncoded(at, out.get(0))); }
  return Ok(out);
}

/**
 * A list of `payload` bytes' worth of items, read until exactly that many are consumed.
 *
 * **`Cursor.sub(payload)` is right here and is not used, which is the finding.** A sub-cursor
 * bounded by the prefix is the operation `@/packages/tls` wanted and the one that makes a
 * length-prefixed format safe by construction — and it would make `ListOverrun` **unreachable**,
 * because a member reading past the list's end would fail against the inner cursor and come back as
 * an overrun. RLP cares about the difference: *the input is short* is a truncated network read and
 * *a member ran past its list's declared end* is an encoder that contradicted itself. So the bound
 * is checked by hand and the sub-cursor is declined.
 *
 * `Vec<Item>` where the original grows an `Item[]` by doubling and then copies it down to size —
 * seventeen lines, and the fifth hand-written grow this exercise has replaced. The original's reason
 * for growing rather than counting stands: *"counting means decoding twice, and the members'
 * lengths are only known by decoding them."*
 */
Result<Item, RlpFault> list(Cursor c, i32 payload) {
  try c.need(payload);
  i32 end = c.at + payload;
  Vec<Item> items = Vec.create();
  while (c.at < end) { items.push(try item(c)); }
  if (c.at != end) { return Err(ListOverrun(end)); }
  return Ok(List(items));
}

/**
 * The length carried by a long-form prefix: `n` big-endian bytes, checked three ways.
 *
 * **`Cursor.be(n)` answers an `i32` and this needs a `u64`**, so the loop stays. Reading up to eight
 * bytes wide is not a refinement here — `TooLong` carries what the input declared, and a prefix that
 * spells 6 GB has to survive being read before it can be refused. §1.
 *
 * **The order changed from the original, and it buys the number in the message.** The original
 * refuses `n > 4` *before* reading the bytes, so the fault it reports cannot say what length was
 * declared — the same sentence for 2^33 and for 2^63.
 */
Result<i32, RlpFault> longLength(Cursor c, i32 n) {
  i32 at = c.at;
  Bytes raw = try c.take(n);
  if (raw.get(0) == 0) { return Err(LeadingZero(at)); }

  u64 v = 0;
  for (i32 i = 0; i < n; i++) { v = (v * 256) + (raw.get(i) as u64); }
  if (v > 2147483647) { return Err(TooLong(at, v)); }

  i32 len = v as@ i32;
  if (len <= 55) { return Err(NotMinimal(at, len)); }
  return Ok(len);
}

Bytes one(u8 b) {
  u8[] a = u8[1]();
  a[0] = b;
  return Bytes.all(a);
}

// ## 1. What the first consumer of `core/cursor.wac` found
//
// The cursor file predicted three costs and one benefit. Scored against a real adoption:
//
// **Predicted, and true: `Overrun` propagates into the fault union.** `./fault.wac` declares
// `union<Overrun, NotSelfEncoded, …> RlpFault` and `Overrun` comes from `core`. That is the first
// union in this directory with a `core` type as a member, and the prediction was that *every* fault
// union in the system would end up naming it. Here it cost nothing, because the member it replaced —
// `Truncated { i32 at; i32 want; i32 have; }` — is `Overrun` field for field, invented independently.
//
// **Predicted, and true: `be(n)` answering an `i32` cannot say its width, and a consumer needs a
// wider one.** `longLength` reads up to eight bytes into a `u64` precisely so `TooLong` can carry a
// length no `i32` holds. The cursor's `be` is unusable here and the hand-written loop stays — which
// is the only operation of the six this file wanted and could not take.
//
// **Predicted, and true: no extension methods.** `item`, `list`, `longLength` and `shortString` were
// methods on this file's own `Reader` and are free functions now, because a package cannot add a
// method to a type it imported. The call sites went from `this.item()` to `item(c)`, which is a
// wash to read and is a real constraint on how far a shared type can go: **the operations that are
// general live on the type and the operations that are a format's live beside it, and the boundary
// is drawn by the language rather than by the design.**
//
// **Not predicted, and the one that matters: the cursor's best operation is the one this consumer
// must decline.** `sub(n)` is what makes a length-prefixed format safe by construction, RLP is a
// length-prefixed format, and using it would delete a member of the fault union that says something
// the format cares about. The cursor file argued `sub` from `@/packages/tls`, where a nested field
// that overruns its prefix and an input that ran out are the same answer. Here they are not.
//
// > **A bound enforced by construction cannot report which bound it was.** That is the general form,
// > and it is the same trade as the trapping one from the other end: the stricter mechanism gives up
// > the ability to say what happened.
//
// ## 2. What could not be written
//
// **`try` in a nested call reads as one expression and is four control-flow edges.**
// `Ok(Str(try c.take(try longLength(c, tag - 0xB7))))` is one line and two early returns, and the
// second `try` only runs if the first succeeded. That is what it means and nothing on the pages says
// so: `GRAMMAR.md` lists *`try` in expression position* with two examples, both a single `try` at the
// head of a statement. The evaluation order of two `try`s in one expression, and whether the outer
// construction is even started, are unstated.
//
// Written this way deliberately rather than split into locals, because the question is worth
// showing. The safe reading — left to right, short-circuit, nothing constructed — is what every
// language with `?` does, and *"what every other language does"* is the argument this directory has
// refused elsewhere.
//
// **A `Vec<Item>` in a recursive enum is a struct in a cycle**, and the original's argument for why
// `Item[]` is finite — *"an array is a reference"* — is about arrays. A `Vec<T>` is a struct holding
// one, so the same reasoning applies and nobody has written it down for the struct case. It is
// exactly the sort of thing that is obviously fine until a compiler computes a size.
//
// **Nothing here needs the position and everything carries it.** Every member of `RlpFault` has an
// `at`, which is right, and the *type* cannot say that they all do. A union of seven structs that
// share a field is seven declarations of that field; an enum with a common payload, or a
// `Positioned<T>` wrapper, would say it once. The first is not a thing wac has and the second makes
// `Err(is LeadingZero)` — the match this directory has been arguing for — stop working, because the
// arm's type is now `Positioned<LeadingZero>`. Recorded as the first place where the two features
// this exercise has been asking for pull against each other.
//
// **`list` constructed an `Overrun` by hand, and that is fixed in the cursor rather than here.**
// `Cursor` had no *would this read fit* that answers the fault — `left()` answers an `i32` — so a
// caller checking a bound itself had to spell the failure the cursor would have spelled. Two
// producers of one value, which `CLAUDE.md` says is one too many. `Cursor.need(n)` exists now and
// `list` writes `try c.need(payload);`, so the only thing this file spells is the bound.
//
// Worth keeping as a sequence rather than as a fix: the gap was invisible while the cursor had no
// consumer, and it took **one** adoption to find it. The shipped `ssh` reader has `need(n)` and
// answers a `bool`; the cursor did not have it at all.
